 "segments": [
  "[  0.00-  1.70] He poured cooking oil into the sea.",
  "[  1.90-  5.38] With a pirate ship so close behind them, he could hear the pirates laughing.",
  "[  5.62-  7.38] His captain grabs him by the collar and goes,",
  "[  7.62-  9.70] We're about to get boarded and you're making soup?",
  "[  9.96- 10.98] But he doesn't even look up.",
  "[ 11.22- 12.62] He just points at the black clouds,",
  "[ 12.88- 13.96] Steer into the storm.",
  "[ 14.22- 16.32] The captain yells that they'll never survive it.",
  "[ 16.42- 18.46] And all he gets back is trust the oil.",
  "[ 18.64- 21.00] But then a grappling hook clangs off the railing.",
  "[ 21.10- 23.98] So the captain turns the wheel and the pirates couldn't be happier.",
  "[ 24.18- 26.60] Why waste a cannonball when the storm will do it for free?",
  "[ 26.86- 29.20] Deeper in, the waves get taller than the mast",
  "[ 29.20- 31.98] and the crew is tying themselves to anything bolted down.",
  "[ 32.22- 34.36] But he's still pouring, a little at a time.",
  "[ 34.56- 36.40] Always on the side, the wind is coming from.",
  "[ 36.68- 39.04] Then the pirates swing in to board and up ahead,",
  "[ 39.24- 42.66] a wave big enough to swallow both ships rises out of the dark.",
  "[ 42.90- 45.56] You see, sailors really carried oil just for this.",
  "[ 45.78- 47.04] They called it storm oil.",
  "[ 47.26- 48.48] Ever blown on a bowl of soup?",
  "[ 48.74- 51.94] The broth ripples, but the oily spots stay perfectly smooth",
  "[ 51.94- 53.48] because your breath can't grab them",
  "[ 53.48- 55.94] and a wave can't crash if the wind can't grab it.",
  "[ 56.02- 58.18] So when the wave reaches their oily patch of sea,",
  "[ 58.18- 60.26] it just lifts the ship and sets it down.",
  "[ 60.42- 62.36] And 50 yards behind, it curls over",
  "[ 62.36- 64.40] and drops straight onto the pirates deck."
 ],
